# Every `resource_type` the audit vocabulary knows, classified by whether it denotes a NATURAL PERSON.
#
# Writers and consumers alike: `AuditResource` is the vocabulary of the write path AND of the erasure and
# query paths, so a type can enter this file through a call that only ever READS rows of it. The registry is
# an inventory of the types the code names, not of the rows it produces. `User` entered that way and no
# longer sits there: the identity erasure names its subject as its compliance entry's resource, so the type
# is written in production and this line closes a live hole rather than reserving one.
#
# Why this file exists: GDPR erasure of the resource axis is the owning bounded context's job
# (docs/adr/audit-activity-log.md D4). That assignment only works if "is this type a person?" is a
# declared, reviewed decision — otherwise a new person-denoting type ships with nobody erasing it, and
# the subject's real id survives their own erasure beside their pseudonym.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   <ResourceType> => non-person
#   <ResourceType> => person :: <path of the erasure use case> :: <path of the witness>
#
# The witness is an acceptance scenario that SEEDS a row of the type and asserts none survives the erasure.
# It is what stops the person line from establishing its own liveness: the erasure owner cannot testify that
# its call reaches a row, and the type's only literal in `src` is the constant that owner itself holds.
#
# The gate is three classes — PersonResourceErasureGateTest over the real tree, plus
# PersonResourceErasureRulesGateTest and PersonResourceErasureWitnessGateTest for the falsifiability of the
# rules — run together by `make php.lint.audit-resource`. It fails the build when a type reaches
# `AuditResource::of(...)` — written as a literal at the call, or held in a constant the call resolves,
# whether declared in the calling class or in another one it imports — or a route's
# `_audit_resource_type` default without a line here; when a `person` line names a file that holds no
# collaborator able to erase the resource axis — an AuditResourceAnonymiser, or the AuditSubjectTrailErasure
# that orders that same pass behind the two-axis row lock — or holds one and never calls its erasing verb;
# when its witness does not exist, is not a `.feature`
# under `features/`, or has no single scenario that both seeds a row of the type and asserts none survives;
# and when a `non-person` type is no longer written anywhere.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it deliberately does NOT do. Read this list as the specification of the gate's blind spots — a green
# build proves what is below the line, and nothing above it.
#
#   - It cannot judge the classification. Calling `Contact` a non-person passes; the completeness check still
#     demands a line, so the decision is forced into a diff, which is the point. Review is what judges it.
#   - `User` IS WRITTEN IN PRODUCTION. FulfilIdentityErasure names the erased subject as its compliance
#     entry's resource, so the person axis of `audit_log` is not empty and this line closes a live hole
#     rather than reserving one. It is the orchestrator and not the identity use case it records, because
#     the statement that clears that identifier is the resource anonymiser it runs next: a use case must
#     not persist an id it has no way of erasing. That writer declares the literal itself and reaches it
#     through `self::`, so this sweep sees it by the same path as any other in-class constant.
#   - The sweep ALSO resolves `OtherClass::CONSTANT` across files, which is how `src` writes it wherever a
#     second use case audits against a type another one declares — `ChangeUserRoles`, `InviteUser` and
#     `ReconcileErasedSubjectReferences` all reach `FulfilIdentityErasure::SUBJECT_RESOURCE_TYPE` that way,
#     precisely so a `'User'` literal cannot mint a second, unregistered carrier of the same type. It earns
#     its cost because the failure it prevents is the worst shape this registry can fail in: an unseen
#     writer means no type in the universe, so no line demanded, so nobody named as obliged to erase it.
#     It no longer fails that way
#     silently either — a constant reference this sweep cannot resolve now raises instead of yielding no
#     type. What does NOT follow is that a green here means "no unseen writer exists", and
#     PersonResourceErasureGateTest says so at the one assertion that used to promise otherwise.
#   - The witness proves the declared erasure path REACHES a row of the type. It does not prove any such row
#     is ever produced, and no check here can: liveness of a producer is precisely what this gate is
#     forbidden to judge.
#   - It cannot tell an assertion that had to be true from one that happened to be. A query whose predicate
#     could never match anything, answered by a zero count, satisfies the witness rule exactly as a real
#     erasure does. What the rule enforces is narrower than it looks: ONE scenario contains both a step that
#     inserts a row naming the type and a query of the type answered by the next step with a zero count. That
#     the two concern the SAME ROW — same id, same table, same column — is a review judgement, not a check.
#   - It reads the witness, it does not RUN it, and nothing here proves the scenario passes. What
#     BehatSuiteCoverageGateTest adds is narrower still: every `.feature` under `api/features` sits beneath a
#     declared suite path, and the default profile declares no filter. It does not establish that Behat ran,
#     and it is reached by `make php.unit` rather than by the quality sweep this gate belongs to.
#   - A seed in a `Background:` block is not attributed to any scenario, so a witness written that way is
#     REFUSED. The alternative — crediting every scenario with it — would let a seed there pair with an
#     assertion in whichever scenario happened to follow, which is the defect the per-scenario rule exists
#     against. Loud and wrong beats quiet and wrong.
#   - The witness seeds its row with hand-written SQL, so it does not detect column drift. A rename of an
#     `audit_log` column would break the scenario at run time, not here, and this file would still read green.
#   - Witness and declaration can be removed in the same commit. Deleting the scenario alone turns the line
#     red, so the pair has to go together — a deliberate, reviewable act rather than an impossible one. Today
#     even that is caught, because dropping the `User` line leaves the type still derived from `src` and the
#     completeness check demands it back; that protection lapses for any type `src` does not name. Note what
#     it rests on: derivation, not deletion. Several files derive `User`, and a rule pins that the erasure
#     owner is one of them rather than leaving it to luck: a `person` type must be BUILT by the file declared
#     to erase it. Refactor that construction away and the build goes red at the owner rule before the
#     completeness one even notices the type has left the universe.
#   - It bounds WHERE a person type may be written, not how many files write it. Every deriving file must sit
#     in the same `src/<Context>/<Module>` as the declared erasure, because D4 assigns the obligation to the
#     owning context and this file has room for exactly one owner per type; siblings inside that module are
#     already covered, since the erasure matches on `(resource_type, resource_id)` and never on who wrote the
#     row. The stricter "exactly one writer" rule this replaces was carrying a CONCURRENCY invariant it could
#     not really hold — see `Shared\Audit\Application\AuditSubjectRowLock`, which now holds it in code — and a
#     file census is the wrong instrument for that: it went red for a correct change, and would have gone
#     green again the moment a writer was deleted for unrelated reasons.
#   - It resolves a constant imported from another class, and does so on the ONE-CLASS-PER-FILE assumption
#     below: `Holder::CONST` is looked up by the holder's SHORT name, which is what a call site spells once
#     the class is imported. Two classes of that short name in different namespaces collapse onto one key,
#     so a lookup can resolve to the wrong sibling's literal and over-include a type. Over-inclusion fails
#     loudly — a line is demanded for a type nothing writes, and the staleness check then refuses it — which
#     is the direction this gate must err in. Resolving imports properly would need a parser.
#   - The source sweep is textual and narrow: single-quoted literals only (double quotes are ruled out by
#     cs-fixer's `single_quote`, not by this gate), `const string NAME = '…'` only for the constant form, and
#     `api/src` only — a writer in a migration, a fixture or a console command outside `src` is not seen.
#   - It cannot see a type assembled at runtime from request attributes; the route-default check covers those
#     inputs instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bank => non-person
BankAccount => non-person
User => person :: src/Iam/Identity/Application/FulfilIdentityErasure.php :: features/backoffice/users/erase.feature
